Adaptive Second-Order Solvers for Fast Stochastic Diffusion Sampling Skip to main content Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Contents Reading mode Colour theme License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2610.03034v1 [cs.LG] 02 Oct 2026 \workshoptitle BeNTo: Beyond Next - Token Prediction Adaptive Second-Order Solvers for Fast Stochastic Diffusion Sampling Ella Kemperman Affiliation: Radboud University Email: ella.kemperman@ru.nl Luca Ambrogioni Affiliation: Radboud University Email: luca.ambrogioni@donders.ru.nl Abstract Diffusion models rely on numerical solvers requiring time-discretization, which has a large influence on the tradeoff between sampling cost and quality. However, the computational difficulty of the reverse process varies along the sampling trajectory and across data distributions, making the choice of discretization important. We adapt proportional-integral (PI) step-size control to diffusion, using our diffusion noise-normalised error estimator. Unlike existing adaptive methods in diffusion that respond only to the current error, the PI solver also incorporates the previous error, yielding smoother step adaptation. We further show that these per-sample trajectories exhibit shared structure and can be aggregated into a fixed schedule that retains much of the benefit of adaptive sampling. We evaluate both approaches on natural-image and language datasets, in terms of quality, measured by FID at a matched number of neural network evaluations (NFE), comparing them with widely used stochastic solvers and schedules. For images, our average discretization outperforms the commonly used EDM schedule in terms of sample quality when used with the stochastic Heun sampler, and with the EDM-churn sampler at low NFE. Additionally, our PI adaptive solver obtains better FID than most stochastic and adaptive baselines, although it does not beat the EDM-churn sampler at low NFE. Moreover, we find our solver outperforms both the EDM and the entropy schedule on language diffusion at low-to-medium NFE in terms of perplexity, with the drawback of lower token entropy. Lastly, we find that the benefit of per-sample adaptivity is problem-dependent. It is highly beneficial in 1D toy examples, while only marginal for image and language data, where the average schedule sometimes even outperforms the per-sample adaptive solver. Code is available at https://github.com/ellakemperman/adaptive-second-order-diffusion-solvers . 1 Introduction Diffusion models generate high-quality images [ 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 ] , videos [ 5 ] , and audio [ 6 ] . However, diffusion sampling is slow, as it is a process requiring a large number of function evaluations (NFE) of a neural network [ 1 , 2 , 7 ] . Song et al. [1] show that the process of diffusion can be treated as solving either an ordinary or stochastic differential equation (ODE/SDE). Recent work [ 8 ] identified critical windows within the integration interval during which many of the generative decisions are made, a phenomenon arising from symmetry-breaking bifurcations in the reverse denoising process [ 9 , 10 ] . This suggests that numerical effort should not be distributed uniformly across the diffusion interval, but rather, more effort should be allocated to the parts of the interval where dynamics are difficult ( 1(a) ), which could improve the NFE-quality tradeoff. Many widely used schedules are handcrafted and calibrated to specific modalities [ 1 , 2 , 3 , 11 ] . Recent work by Stancevic et al. [12] discussed constructing these schedules in a data-driven manner by enforcing a linear progression in entropy. However, the authors find that their schedules are effective only for first-order samplers, with performance degrading when used with second-order methods. This limits the applicability of such schedules, as second-order samplers have been shown to improve the NFE–quality trade-off in diffusion models [ 2 ] . Thus, we require a method of generating schedules in a data-driven manner that also functions for second-order samplers. Numerical analysis literature offers an alternative that satisfies both conditions: adaptive solvers [ 13 , 14 , 15 , 16 , 17 , 18 ] , which adapt step-size based on the local difficulty, allowing smaller step-sizes along more difficult regions. Additionally, these are generally agnostic to solver-order, allowing them to generate second-order schedules. This allows the schedule to differ on a per-sample basis, and removes the need for handcrafting. Application of these adaptive solvers to diffusion remains limited, though not unexplored [ 2 , 7 ] . Jolicoeur-Martineau et al. [7] devise an adaptive SDE solver specifically designed for diffusion, adapting step-size based on the current local-error. We instead adapt stochastic proportional-integral (PI) control for step-size adaptation [ 16 ] , which incorporates the previous local error alongside the current one, giving smoother step-size adaptation than integral-only control [ 19 ] . We adapt its error estimator to normalise by diffusion noise, substantially improving its performance in diffusion sampling. We compare our method to existing diffusion sampler-schedule combinations in terms of NFE-quality tradeoff. Additionally, we show that the per-sample trajectories have shared structure, which can be exploited by aggregating the trajectories into a single static schedule, usable as any other handcrafted schedule (an example of this is shown in 1(b) ). We evaluate its performance and compare it with the per-sample adaptive solver to determine if per-sample adaptivity is beneficial. (a) (b) Figure 1 : The difficulty of generative diffusion differs across time, requiring different step-sizes at different time-points, which our PI solver determines (a). These per-sample paths share structure and can then be aggregated into an average path usable for sampling (b). 2 Related Works Diffusion Samplers Many improvements to diffusion sampling without retraining have been made since diffusion models were introduced [ 20 , 11 ] . Song et al. [1] introduce the Predictor-Corrector sampler, alternating predictor and corrector steps. Zhao et al. [21] devise a unified predictor-corrector framework called UniPC. A different line of work looks into higher-order solvers. Karras et al. [2] propose a second-order solver using Heun’s method, finding that it sits optimally at the NFE-quality tradeoff, and introduce a stochastic sampler that temporarily increases noise at each step before denoising (churn). Lu et al. [22] , Lu et al. [23] develop an efficient higher-order solver, using the insight that the drift part of the DEs can be analytically solved. To summarize, extensive work has gone into improving diffusion samplers and improving the sampling technique can substantially improve sample quality at low NFE. Unlike these works, this paper focuses on time-step selection using adaptive step-size control for second-order samplers, rather than the specific numerical integration method used. Diffusion Discretization Determining the optimal discretizations for the probability flow ODE and reverse SDE has been an active topic of research after Song et al. [1] introduced the SDE framework and two handcrafted discretizations. Karras et al. [2] devise a new way of parametrizing the probability flow ODE and handcraft a new discretization (EDM). Besides handcrafting, there are methods that obtain discretizations in a principled way. Sabour et al. [24] introduce Align Your Steps, a technique that is grounded in stochastic calculus, obtaining optimized discretizations based on the solver used and the dataset. Chen et al. [25] similarly optimize the time discretization, using gradient descent. Stancevic et al. [12] discretize time in a way that is linearly spaced in terms of entropy, which can be reliably estimated from the score matching loss. However, the performance of their schedule drops for second-order samplers. Moreover, there are techniques that aim to optimize time-discretizations in an empirical way. Xia et al. [26] train a simple model that predicts the next time step based on a sample 𝐱 t \mathbf{x}_{t} and a time t t . Huang et al. [27] take a reinforcement learning approach, treating time-step scheduling as a control problem. They use this approach to determine a data-driven time discretization, which they show can be interpolated and extrapolated across different NFE. However, they only show their technique to work for first-order solvers. In contrast to these approaches, this work does not directly optimize the discretization based on a predefined objective. Instead, discretizations are determined per sample and per step, using a second-order adaptive solver with PI control, and these are aggregated into one static discretization. Adaptive SDE Samplers A substantial amount of work in the field of numerical analysis has gone into improving SDE sampling with adaptive solvers, as many different fields benefit from this. Mauthner [17] develop a technique that uses an embedded Stochastic Runge-Kutta method to determine the local error, using that error to control the step-size. Ilie et al. [16] expand on this approach, introducing an adaptive solver using PI control specifically designed for multidimensional SDEs, taking inspiration from Gustafsson et al. [19] and adding a component incorporating the previous error. This approach forms the basis of the adaptive solver we apply to generative diffusion. The closest work in diffusion literature is by Jolicoeur-Martineau et al. [7] . They devise an adaptive stochastic solver and apply it to diffusion, using the difference between the first- and second-order estimates as an error metric. Their work differs from this work in the step-size controller used, only using a component proportional to the current error, not considering previous error. 3 Background A diffusion process progressivel