d with a static schedule generated by the PI solver using the method described in section 5 . The experiment was performed on the variance-preserving SDE [ 1 ] , using an analytical expression of the score function. We measure the Wasserstein distance at varying NFE. For the PI solver, τ r ​ e ​ l = 0 	au_{rel}=0 was used, and τ a ​ b ​ s 	au_{abs} was used to control NFE. For details on the experimental implementation, see Appendix D , and for the hyperparameters, see Appendix C . 2(a) shows that the PI solver outperforms the other solvers by a large margin at low NFE. When NFE increases, the benefit over stochastic Heun’s method with a linear schedule decreases. This can be explained by both samplers reaching the minimum Wasserstein distance, with PI converging more quickly. Stochastic Heun’s with a PI static time schedule performs well at very low NFE ranges, but performance quickly degrades. 2(b) shows how our solver divides computational effort over the time interval. Note that there is substantial variance in the individual allocation. This likely explains the poor performance of the stochastic Heun with PI static configuration, and suggests that per-sample adaptivity is beneficial for this problem. (a) (b) Figure 2 : The Wasserstein distance against the NFE for different SDE solvers applied to diffusion on a mixture of Gaussians (a). The cumulative computational effort allocated ( i N  rac{i}{N} ) plotted against diffusion time t t at τ a ​ b ​ s ≈ 0.02656 	au_{abs}\approx 0.02656 applied to diffusion on a Gaussian mixture (b). 6.2 FFHQ and ImageNet We evaluated the PI solver and the schedules it generates on FFHQ [ 31 ] and ImageNet 64x64 [ 32 ] . We compare it to the Euler-Maruyama [ 29 ] , stochastic Heun [ 30 ] , EDM-churn [ 2 ] , and the Gotta Go Fast (named after their paper) [ 7 ] solvers. Notably, for each of these solvers, we run the steps from t o ​ d ​ e t_{ode} onwards on the probability flow ODE. The SDE parametrization detailed in Appendix A was