 step trajectories into a static discretization. Our solver can be used to generate second-order compatible schedules in a data-driven manner, and these improve on the existing handcrafted EDM schedule in several of our evaluated settings. Limitations While we did evaluate the influence of τ r ​ e ​ l 	au_{rel} and τ a ​ b ​ s 	au_{abs} on the NFE-quality tradeoff, no full search over the hyperparameter space was conducted. Moreover, all image and language experiments were conducted using VE models. The 1D experiments were done with a VP SDE and show a significant benefit of adaptivity. It is unclear if this is a property of the VP SDE or of the specific dataset. Additionally, these experiments were only conducted for stochastic sampling. Preliminary testing indicates the PI solver can also be applied to deterministic sampling, but it is left to future work to investigate if it outperforms existing sampler-schedule combinations. This could also allow the solver to discover the final ODE steps by itself, instead of requiring an existing static schedule for the last steps. 8 AI Use Statement In this work, we have used generative AI to provide feedback on the research methodology, asking it to specifically highlight potentially important additional ablations. Moreover, we used it to provide feedback on the manuscript in terms of writing style and consistency. Lastly, we used AI to help with identifying the cause of bugs in the code. Generative AI was not used for any other purposes in this paper. All of the feedback provided by LLMs was first manually checked for correctness and usefulness before applying the feedback. No AI-generated code ended up in the final codebase, so AI-generated code did not have to be checked for correctness. We take responsibility for the final content of this work, including text, claims or artifacts produced with the aid of generative AI. 9 Reproducibility Statement To make the paper reproducible, we provide a link to the github repository 